AUDIOCTL(1)                     User Commands                      AUDIOCTL(1)

NAME
       audioctl - control the volume, outputs and streams of the audio service

SYNOPSIS
       audioctl [-q] [--json] [status]
       audioctl [-q] [--json] volume [N|+N|-N|N%|L,R|up|down|max|min]
       audioctl [-q] [--json] mute | unmute | toggle
       audioctl [-q] [--json] output [auto|all|TYPE|ID|NAME]
       audioctl [--json] outputs | streams | cards | info
       audioctl [-q] [--json] stream-volume ID N[,R]
       audioctl [-q] kill ID...
       audioctl [-q] card N
       audioctl [--json] watch [COUNT]

DESCRIPTION
       audioctl talks to the audio service, audiod(1), which mixes  the  sound
       of every program and drives the sound card. With it the  master  volume
       and mute are set,  the  output  is  chosen  --  the  built-in  speaker,
       headphones, a line output or an HDMI or DisplayPort monitor -- and  the
       streams that are playing now are listed, made  louder  or  quieter,  or
       ended.

       It never opens the sound device itself.  Everything  goes  through  the
       service's socket, which every local user may use, so audioctl needs  no
       privilege; the service decides what a request may change. Any user  may
       set the master volume, the mute and the output. A stream may be changed
       or ended by the user who opened it and by root. Only  root  may  switch
       the service to another sound card.

       When the volume, the mute or the output  changes,  audioctl  plays  the
       short system sound "volume" so the new level can be heard at  once.  -q
       suppresses it.

       Without a command audioctl prints a summary: the card  and  codec,  the
       format the mixer runs in, the master volume and mute, the outputs  with
       their jack state and the streams.

COMMANDS
       status
              The summary described above. This is the default.

       volume [VALUE]
              Without VALUE, print the master volume. Otherwise set it.  VALUE
              is a number 0..100 (a trailing % is allowed), +N or -N to change
              it relative to the current level, L,R (or L:R) to  set  the  two
              sides apart, up or down for a step of 5, and max or min for  100
              and 0. A relative change moves both sides by the same amount, so
              a balance set with L,R is kept until one side reaches an end.

              Turning the volume up while the output is muted also unmutes it.

       mute, unmute, toggle
              Mute or unmute the master output, or flip the current state. The
              volume setting is kept while muted.

       output [CHOICE]
              Without CHOICE, print  the  output  mode  and  which  output  is
              active. Otherwise choose where sound goes:

              auto -- the headphones while something  is  plugged  into  their
              jack, the speaker  otherwise.  This  is  the  default,  and  the
              service follows the jack by itself.

              all -- every output that is present at once.

              speaker, headphone, line, hdmi, dp, spdif, headset -- the  first
              output of that kind that has  something  plugged  in,  else  the
              first of that kind. hdmi also takes a DisplayPort  monitor  when
              there is no HDMI one, since both are  simply  "the  monitor"  to
              most people.

              An output ID or NAME as listed by audioctl outputs, for  example
              2 or "HDMI 1".

              Choosing an output with nothing plugged in is  allowed,  with  a
              warning: the jack may not report a device that is there.

       outputs
              List the outputs: an asterisk marks the active  ones;  then  the
              id, the name, the kind, whether something is plugged in  ("built
              in" for a speaker) and, for HDMI and DisplayPort, the  monitor's
              own name when the monitor reported one.

       streams
              List the streams playing now:  their  id,  role  (music,  video,
              game,  event,  phone,  web,  other),  state  (playing,   paused,
              draining, underrun), format, volume, how long they have  played,
              their name, the program that opened them, its user  and  process
              id.

       stream-volume ID N[,R]
              Set the volume of one stream, 0..100, with  the  same  forms  as
              volume (+N and -N are relative to the stream's current  volume).
              The stream keeps playing; only its share of the mix changes.

       kill ID...
              End one or more streams. The program that owned the stream  sees
              its connection closed.

       cards  List the sound cards the service knows. An  asterisk  marks  the
              one in use.

       card [N]
              Without N, print the card in use. With N, make the service drive
              card N instead. Only root may do this: it changes the  sound  of
              every user.

       info   Everything the service reports about itself:  protocol  version,
              uptime, the number of streams, the  device  underruns  since  it
              started, the rate, channel count and format the mixer  runs  at,
              the period size and count of the device buffer and  the  latency
              they make, and the card in use.

       watch [COUNT]
              Subscribe to the service's change notifications and  print  each
              as it arrives, with the time: volume and  mute  changes,  output
              changes (also those the jack causes),  streams  that  start  and
              end,  and  cards  that  come   and   go.   Stops   after   COUNT
              notifications, on Ctrl-C, or when the service  goes  away  (exit
              status 1).

OPTIONS
       Options go before the command; everything after the command belongs  to
       it, so that audioctl volume -5 means "5 quieter".

       -q, --quiet
              Print nothing on success and play no feedback sound. Errors  are
              still reported.

       -j, --json
              Print the result as one line of JSON instead of text: an  object
              for status, volume, output and info, an array  for  streams  and
              cards, and one object per line for watch. Strings  are  escaped;
              names come from other programs  and  are  never  trusted  to  be
              printable. Text output replaces control characters in names with
              ?.

       -h, --help
              Print a summary of the commands.

       -V, --version
              Print the version of the library and protocol.

EXAMPLES
       audioctl volume 40          set the master volume to 40%
       audioctl volume +10         ten louder
       audioctl -q volume down     quieter by 5, silently (for key bindings)
       audioctl toggle             mute or unmute
       audioctl output hdmi        sound to the monitor
       audioctl output auto        back to following the headphone jack
       audioctl streams            what is playing
       audioctl stream-volume 12 30

EXIT STATUS
       0      Success.

       1      The service could not  be  reached,  refused  the  request  (for
              example a stream that belongs to another user, or a card  change
              by a user other than root), or the stream, output or  card  does
              not exist.

       2      A bad command or argument.

FILES
       /var/run/audiod/socket
              The service's socket.

       /usr/share/sounds/likeos/volume.wav
              The feedback sound.

SEE ALSO
       audiod(1), audioplay(1), audiotest(1), audioinfo(1), eventsound(1)

